Britain has always been a maritime  nation linked to the rest of the world  by the Seas and rivers that feed us and  now Sky News has learned that in future  Goods coming into British waters may  face fewer charges than ever before  an intentional echo of Britain's history  as a trading nation a history that  begins in this place for centuries this  was the epicenter of British trade and  if you were bringing goods into the  country you would have to take them here  custom-house you'd come up this  staircase having more drillship on the  river outside you would go through this  corridor with all of the details of what  you were importing whether it was food  spices plus textiles and you would take  them in here this it's the long room and  here you'd go up to the counter over  there you'd pay your duties to the  officials and only then could you sell  those goods on to the merchants who were  thronging around this room today the  tariffs aren't processed on written  Ledger's and books everything's done  electronically but the same principle is  in place when goods enter this country  they pay a tariff a kind of tax for the  past 45 years those tariffs were decided  by the EU but when the UK leaves  especially without a deal it may  suddenly be able to decide them all over  again so why does that matter for the  rest of us because so much of what you  buy in the shops has to come from  overseas which means it faces tariffs  and that might be good news for  producers here in the UK but it's bad  news potentially for shoppers because  higher towers mean higher prices and  that's why the government is considering  in the event of a no deal brexit cutting  those towers in fact across the board to  give you an idea of what we're talking  about this shows you the UK schedule of  tariffs every single item coming into  this country and Whitehall sources tell  me the plan is to cut 80 to 90 percent  of those tariffs down to zero it would  represent the single biggest  liberalization of trade in British  history some goods will be protected  lamb and beef imports will still have  big tariffs to safeguard farmers but  most foods for instance the cereals used  to make bread and snacks like crisps are  expected to see their tariffs cut to  zero car imports will still face a ten  percent  but the tariffs on car parts will be  slashed it's a difficult balancing act  tariffs always have the potential for  controversy between producer interests  consumer interests even between  different producers between people who  use products to make end products this  has the potential to be really  controversial in most countries and  we've avoided much of that in the UK  this 21st century custom schedule is  still a closely guarded secret in  Whitehall and if there's a deal next  week it may never see the light of day  but it is the first hint of the answer  to the biggest question of all what is  this maritime nation's economic destiny  after it leaves the EU Eddie Conway Sky  News
 

